\textbf{Per-task concentration.} Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla; overall CAR is dominated by three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6; Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}). Detach is systematically worst on these hard tasks (T3: 0.11, T5: 0.05), consistent with gradient collapse exacerbating degradation where obstacle proximity is tight.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla.} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039, identical to Vanilla, despite providing per-pixel geometric information. Three label types (structured BEV, random binary, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained.

\textbf{Temporal dynamics (preliminary).} Cutoff@35K (3 seeds) achieves CAR 0.779, statistically indistinguishable from Vanilla ($\Delta = -$0.1\,pp), which confirms that 35K steps of gradient flow fully protects collision avoidance. Surprisingly, Cutoff@25K (0.724, 2 seeds) and Reverse Cutoff (0.718, 2 seeds) fall substantially \emph{below} Vanilla. The most parsimonious explanation: setting the loss weight to zero retains the auxiliary head forward pass, so the post-cutoff period operates in Detach mode; BatchNorm interference acts on representations shaped by gradient flow, without gradient compensation. This is not withdrawal from gradient flow \emph{per se}, but transition into an actively harmful regime. Third seeds are in progress; extended analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:temporal}.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes TSR and CAR across conditions (50 rollouts $\times$ 10 tasks per seed). Statistical comparisons use paired $t$-tests and Cohen's $d$ against the Vanilla baseline.

\textbf{Crossover interaction between normalization and gradient flow.} The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) reveal a crossover interaction rather than additive effects (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}). Full OCC with BN maintains CAR comparable to Vanilla (0.797 vs.\ 0.780, $d{=}+0.69$, $p{=}0.44$). Detach OCC with BN falls 3.4\,pp below Vanilla ($p{=}0.031$, $d{=}-3.90$), the only statistically significant pairwise difference. Detach+LN (0.765) recovers 1.9\,pp of this deficit by eliminating BatchNorm interference. However, Full OCC+LN (0.743) consistently degrades CAR by 3.7\,pp below Vanilla despite maintaining gradient flow; this is the worst configuration tested. BN is thus beneficial when gradient flow calibrates its statistics, but harmful when gradient flow is absent; LN shows the opposite pattern. These results are consistent with the hypothesis that label content does not affect outcomes: Random OCC (0.789), Depth (0.780), and Full OCC (0.797) are all within cross-seed variance ($p > 0.4$).

\textbf{Detach destabilizes optimization broadly.} Detach OCC exhibits TSR variance an order of magnitude above other conditions ($\sigma = 0.163$ vs.\ $< 0.050$): one seed collapses to TSR 0.624 with uniform degradation across all tasks, while its CAR (0.748) stays comparable to the other two seeds. The low CAR variance ($\sigma = 0.002$) despite volatile TSR ($\sigma = 0.163$) suggests deterministic convergence to a degraded safety behavior: BN interference imposes a consistent representational distortion regardless of initialization.



