\subsection{Normalization--Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}). A $2{\times}2$ ANOVA confirms a significant interaction ($F(1,8){=}7.511$, $p{=}0.025$, partial $\eta^2{=}0.484$, Cohen's $f{=}0.969$), while neither main effect reaches significance (gradient flow: $p{=}0.326$; normalization: $p{=}0.231$). The interaction effect is 7.3\,pp: gradient flow's effect on CAR reverses sign depending on normalization type.

\textbf{Crossover, not additive.} With BN, gradient flow improves CAR by $+$5.1\,pp (Full OCC 0.797 vs.\ Detach 0.746; simple effect $p{=}0.052$, $d{=}5.13$). With LN, gradient flow \emph{degrades} CAR by $-$2.2\,pp (Full OCC+LN 0.743 vs.\ Detach+LN 0.765; $p{=}0.26$). Neither simple effect reaches significance individually, but the large effect sizes and opposing directions confirm the crossover pattern. This rules out an additive decomposition: gradient flow benefits are BatchNorm-mediated, arising from gradient calibration of BN running statistics, not from an intrinsic regularization property of auxiliary gradients.

\textbf{Full OCC+LN is the worst configuration.} Despite maintaining gradient flow and using a normalization layer that eliminates representational drift (Section~\ref{sec:gradient_dynamics}), Full OCC+LN (0.743) falls 3.7\,pp below Vanilla, consistently across all three seeds (0.766, 0.718, 0.744). This is the strongest evidence for a representation-behavior dissociation: the configuration that best preserves pretrained representations produces the worst safety behavior.

